This patch is needed because Solaris home directories can be NFS-backed, and
SQLite WAL mode can fail during startup when it tries to mmap the `-shm`
sidecar.

What it does:
- keeps WAL mode for Codex state databases on local Solaris filesystems
- detects NFS-backed SQLite homes with statvfs(2) and uses rollback journaling
- enables upstream's exclusive staged WAL conversion on Solaris NFS
- reuses upstream migration locking, backups and interrupted-cutover recovery
- stages database and WAL without SHM before opening the normal pool
- adds a Solaris regression test for enforcement without managed configuration
- excludes two tests that require descriptor-scoped flock/directory write locks
- keeps WAL mode unchanged on other targets

Why it is needed:
- opening an existing WAL database in place can map `-shm` before a
  `PRAGMA journal_mode=DELETE` request is processed
- that ordering fails on Solaris NFS with `SQLITE_IOERR_SHMMAP` / extended
  error code 5386, so conversion must complete on a local filesystem first

Upstream now provides NFS migration, but its filesystem probe is Linux-only
and existing WAL conversion otherwise requires managed opt-in. Extend the
shared probe with statvfs(2) and enable conversion by default on Solaris.
Migration staging honors TMPDIR; use local disk, not a large RAM-backed /tmp.

Related upstream issue: https://github.com/openai/codex/issues/30957

